1. Introduction
Challenges in the design of cooling systems for liquid rocket engines are unique because of the harsh environment in terms of flow and material temperatures, heat flux level and flow pressure [1]. Despite the acquired experience, the technological advancements for the realization of rocket thrust chambers allow the selection of innovative designs and materials as well as new liquid propellants as coolants. One of the keys to the success of a new technology is the capability to realize safe and efficient cooling systems, requiring adequate physical understanding of the phenomena and effects associated with the selection of a given material or design. In this respect, crucial support is provided by numerical simulations. The temperature distribution within the solid walls of a cooling jacket and the resulting heat fluxes strongly affect the system performance and material resistance, especially if reusable systems are envisaged. Moreover, the wall temperature is strictly dependent on the thermal conductivity of the wall materials and on the channel geometry. The resulting coupling between the temperature field inside the walls and the heat exchange capabilities of the coolant flow makes the numerical solution of the coolant
