the fluid phase. The RANS closure under scrutiny is the oneequation model of Spalart and Allmaras which is often used as a trade-off between simplicity and predictive capability. RANS simulations are conducted by a solver which was previously validated against experimental data for supercritical pressure coolant in a heated channel [2,8]. The paper is organized as follows. In Section 2 we illustrate the set-up for the CHT test cases; in Sections 3 and 4 we provide details about the RANS and the DNS solvers, respectively; results are presented in Section 5, and concluding remarks are given in Section 6.
2. Definition of test cases
We study CHT in a straight cooling channel with rectangular cross section and smooth walls, subjected to a uniform heat flux at its lower side, herein referred to as liner (Fig. 1). In order to facilitate comparison of numerical results, the coolant is assumed to be an incompressible fluid with uniform and isotropic properties (density, viscosity, specific heat, and thermal conductivity), hence the relationship between heat flux and the temperature difference between wall and bulk temperature is linear. The solid wall is also assumed as a material with uniform and isotropic properties (conductivity, specific heat, density). As a result, both the heat transfer coefficient and the pressure gradient are independent of the input heat flux, once the flow is fully developed. The main geometrical properties describing the channel cross section are given in Fig. 2, in which the hydraulic diameter is assumed as the reference length. The channel length is assumed to be either 240 Dh or 480 Dh in the (space-developing) RANS simulations, and 6 Dh in DNS, in which a fully developed flow is assumed on account of streamwise periodicity. Note that, despite the general validity of the present analysis for heated rectangular channels, we prefer to follow the nomenclature of rocket cooling channels because of the framework of the present research.
