3.1. Grid convergence
A grid refinement study for the test case geometry has been carried out. The assumed channel length is L/Dh = 240. The number of cells of the coarse, reference, and fine grids are reported in Fig. 3, indicating the streamwise, horizontal and vertical grid resolution in the three spatial directions, respectively, for both the flow and wall regions. The resolution of the different grids is obtained while keeping the same clustering laws. Therefore, the resulting values of the dimensionless wall distance y+ changes according to the grid level. Except the inlet region, the maximum value of y+ of a wall cell occurring for each grid is about 3.5, 1.0, and 0.5 for the coarse, reference, and fine grids, respectively. Values of order one, which are required to solve up to the wall, are thus obtained for all grids except the coarsest one. Note that the maximum value of y+ is significantly higher than the average on the bottom and side walls for each of the solved grids. A first grid convergence analysis has been carried out on the main bulk properties: temperature and pressure. In Figs. 4(a), and 4(b) the evolution of the bulk temperature and the coolant pressure along the channel is reported for the three grids. Values are nondimensionalized with the inlet temperature Ti and with the exit pressure pe, respectively. Fig. 4(a) shows that no effect of the mesh on the result of bulk temperature is detected. This is an expected result indicating that the energy balance is correctly satisfied, independently of resolution. On the other hand, the results reported in Fig. 4(b) for pressure show a visible displacement at the coarsest levels. However, the changes between reference and fine grid are quite smaller, such that the adoption of the fine mesh in place of the reference one is not justified. Overall, the reference grid features on the one hand a good resolution at the wall to allow for solving the boundary layer up to the wall (i.e., y+ order one) and on the other hand the final solution quite close to that of the fine grid. It is for this reason
that Grid B has been selected as the reference grid for the studies carried out in Section 5. A further analysis of grid convergence has been done on the evaluation of overall friction (Fig. 5(a)) and heat transfer coefficients (Fig. 5(b)). Friction factor and Nusselt number are presented as their ratio to the corresponding theoretical (empirical) value obtained by Eqs. 2 and 17, respectively. The friction factor behavior is based on the derivative of pressure and therefore it clearly follows what can be seen in Fig. 4(b). Fig. 5(a) allows to appreciate an almost second-order convergence of the value of fD which quickly reaches its asymptotic value. Note that the asymptotic value, starting from a value higher than the one from the correlation, converges towards a value clearly lower than theoretical. The final value obtained by Richardson extrapolation [15] is also reported in Fig. 5(a) and corresponds to fD = 0.0240. The Nusselt number shows a similar behavior, quickly reaching the asymptotic value. Its behavior in the ISOTH case shows a second-order behavior Fig. 5(b). The final value obtained by Richardson extrapolation [15] is also reported in Fig. 5(b) as the extrapolated dashed line and corresponds to Nu = 135.6. Note that the asymptotic value of the Nusselt number is significantly lower than the one of the correlation, and this shows the importance to discuss the present results with DNS solutions as will be done in Section 5. A similar behavior is found for the CHT cases, with a slightly lower order of convergence due to the coupling between RANS and Fourier solvers and to the longer channels required to reach the fully developed regime.
3.2. Flow development
Despite the results of the grid convergence analysis show that the main global parameters of interest, i.e friction factor and Nusselt number reach their asymptotic values within about 50 and 100 hydraulic diameters from the entrance, indicating that fully developed flow conditions are reached, a further analysis is carried out to verify that developed flow condition are reached locally. To this aim, the nondimensional velocity and temperature profiles are analyzed in the following. The analysis of the velocity profiles for Grid A allows to verify the hydraulic flow development along the channel. In Fig. 6, the velocity profiles are shown on the vertical and horizontal geometrical symmetry planes (z = tr/2 in the top panel and y = 0 in the bottom panel). It is found that the velocity profiles are practically unaltered at axial positions downstream of x ∼= 50 Dh. Both at the planes of half width and half height of the channel, it is clearly seen that the velocity profile is unchanged between 120 and 240 hydraulic diameters from the entrance. The solution therefore shows a fully developed velocity profile at the end of the channel. As we have seen in the above discussion, the behavior of temperature and velocity parameters are similar in the ISOTH case. We have also anticipated that asymmetrically heated CHT simulations require a longer length to reach full thermal development. The behavior of the temperature development is therefore shown for the LOWCOND case to highlight the delayed temperature field development. The analysis is therefore carried out for a longer channel. In particular, Fig. 7 is obtained with the same number of cells at the cross section as Grid B but twice the number of cells in x-direction and twice the length of the channel. The results are shown also for temperature as profiles on the vertical and horizontal geometrical symmetry planes (z = tr/2 in the top panel and y = 0 in the bottom panel). It is interesting to highlight that there are still