The first comparison addresses the evaluation of velocity field and of the associated friction coefficient, as obtained from simulations and from established empirical relations. The analysis of friction is not repeated for all the flow cases because, owing to the assumption of constant fluid and solid properties, the momentum equation is fully uncoupled from the temperature field. Hence, the following data, taken from the LOWCOND flow case are identical to those obtained for the ISOTH and HIGHCOND cases. Maps of the instantaneous axial velocity field in a cross-stream plane computed with DNS are shown in Fig. 8, at three representative time instants. For the sake of interpretation, the blue zone surrounding the channel marks the solid domain, where the velocity is zero. As expected, the velocity field exhibits significant deviations from the averaged state, with the presence of low-speed fluid intrusions from the wall to the core region, and conversely high-speed fluid sweeps toward the wall. The axial velocity distributions from RANS (obtained with grid B) and DNS are compared In Fig. 9. In both cases, the maximum velocity exceeds the bulk value by about 25%. However, the contour lines away from walls tend to be more rounded in RANS, whereas in DNS they tend to more closely conform to the duct shape. As a result, the velocities near the duct corners tend to be higher in the DNS. This difference may be attributed to the inability of RANS based on the linear eddy-viscosity ansatz (and in particular, of the Spalart-Allmaras model) to predict secondary eddies, thus inhibiting additional momentum and heat transfer from the duct center toward the corners [21]. This interpretation is supported by the visualization of the mean streamlines in the channel cross section obtained in DNS, as shown in Fig. 10. Four pairs of asymmetric rollers are observed which very nearly satisfy the symmetry constraints imposed by the duct shape, and whose effect is bringing high-speed fluid from the core flow to the corners to compensate for momentum defect. The question is whether this large topological change translates to differences in terms of the friction and heat transfer coefficients of engineering interest. Indeed, it has been recently shown [19], that the effect of the secondary motions on the friction coefficient in a square duct is quite small, and quantifiable in terms of a few percent variation. Hence, while secondary motions yield a large change in the distribution of the friction coefficient along walls, their artificial suppression is not expected to yield substantial changes in the global flow properties. Next, in Table 2 we compare the friction factors obtained from RANS and DNS with those predicted from the correlation (2). In the case of square ducts, the correlation (2) yields predictions of the friction coefficient which are accurate to within 0.2% of the DNS results [19]. Here, the 4.5% difference with respect to DNS is due to the rectangular shape of the cross section, which is not properly accounted for by the correlation. This is also in line with the reported uncertainty of about ±5% when the correlation (2) is applied to cross-sectional shapes other than circular [9]. Despite inability to capture the secondary motions, the friction coefficient obtained with RANS is only about 1.6% less than the DNS estimate, hence in line with expectations [18].
