6. Discussion and conclusions
Continuous technological innovations in space propulsion and particularly in liquid rocket engines, require adequate and innovative means to provide support in the development phase. In this paper we consider the prototype case of heat exchanger consisting of a rectangular channel, under the simplifying assumption of uniform and isotropic properties of fluid and solid media. Two conjugate heat transfer approaches have been compared, one based on the coupled solution of the RANS and Fourier equations for the fluid and solid domains, and the second based on seamless DNS of the Navier-Stokes equations and of the solid domain. The RANS closure selected in the present study is based on the Spalart-Allmaras one-equation model because of its simplicity and widespread use. As a consequence, the discussion is strictly related to this model, while specific simulations should be carried out to discuss the behavior of different RANS closures. The results herein presented for conjugate heat transfer with different values of the solid-to-fluid conductivity ratio unveil a number of important issues, and pinpoint discrepancies of correlations of standard use, as well as some difference between RANS and DNS results. First of all, we find that the discrepancy of reference correlations with respect to DNS data is within expectations in the ISOTH case (infinite solid conductivity). According to the literature, errors within ±9% are expected for non-circular ducts with uniform heating [10]. In fact, we find that difference of the fluid Nusselt number estimated by the Gnielinski analogy with respect to DNS is negligible in the ISOTH case. Deviations are larger when finite solid conductivity is accounted for. In general, the effect of finite solid conductivity is a strongly asymmetric, yet distributed, heat load on the fluid, as most heat transfer becomes concentrated at the liner wall. In this respect, we note that the study of rectangular ducts under asymmetric heat load conditions has been rarely addressed in the past, despite its great practical relevance [27,28]. No general conclusion seems to arise from past studies, and in fact the general recommendation which is given in heat transfer handbooks is to use the same estimates as for uniform heating [10]. Here, both DNS and RANS consistently suggest that in the presence of finite wall conductivity the global fluid Nusselt number decreases by decreasing the solid conductivity. Specifically, reductions with respect to the ISOTH case amount to about 12% in DNS, and 17% in RANS, which is quite substantial. Decrease of the thermal efficiency of the fluid as a result of finite conductivity within the solid is here explained as a result of the thermal stratification induced by the finite wall thermal conductivity, whereby only the lower part of the channel is taking part in the convective heat transfer, whereas mean temperature gradients in the upper part of the duct are very small. This effect is found to be monotonic with the k f /ks parameter. Consistent with general claims [27], we find that the Nusselt number at the most heated (liner) wall decreases as compared to the isothermal case, however the values predicted by DNS and RANS/Fourier are (see Figs. 17 and 21) Nul = 128 and Nul = 104, respectively, quite far from the only known specific correlation for rectangular ducts with heating on one side only [28], which instead would yield an unrealistically high Nul = 176. Despite inherent limitation of the RANS model used here, which relies on linear eddy-viscosity parametrization of the turbulent shear stress, hence precluding the appearance of secondary eddies, we find that RANS does a good job in predicting pressure losses within the duct, with O(1%) error. The main qualitative difference with respect to DNS is a more rounded distribution of the friction and heat transfer coefficients, which are nearly flat in the DNS. Differences in the heat transfer performance of the duct are larger than for friction, from 10% in the ISOTH case to 17% in the LOWCOND case. Discrepancies between RANS and DNS in predic
tion of the fluid Nusselt number translate into differences in the prediction of the mean liner wall temperature, which is higher in RANS/Fourier. Increasing accuracy of the correlation (22) in predicting the liner wall temperature is a symptom of reduced importance of thermal diffusion through the rib walls, which is in fact supported by analysis of the temperature fields. The most important practical information in CHT is the global heat transfer coefficient of the system Nug as defined in Eq. (18), which expresses in non-dimensional form the ratio between the imposed heat flux through the bottom of the liner wall and the resulting temperature difference with respect to the fluid bulk. Equation (19) suggests that the overall performance of the CHT system is proportional to the fluid Nusselt number (Nuc) and to the ratio of the difference between the mean duct wall temperature and bulk temperature, and the total temperature difference (expressed by θ w,c). This expression suggests a two-fold effect of conjugate heat transfer, which indirectly affects Nuc as discussed below, and also directly affects θ w,c. In this sense, the ISOTH case is optimal, as it yields both optimal Nuc owing to uniform heating conditions, and maximum θ w,c = 1. Finite conductivity of the solid walls yields mild decrease of Nuc, as well as large decrease of the mean peripheral temperature along the duct walls, resulting in large decrease of Nug, which is well visible in Table 3. When all the effects are lumped together, our model based on a coupled RANS/Fourier approach does an overall good job, with a maximum error of about 10% in the ISOTH case, reducing to less than 5% in the LOWCOND case as a result of the greater relative importance of conductive heat transfer as compared to the convective one. We believe that the data reported in this paper may contribute to the development of advanced prediction methods for cooling channels of rocket engines, which should be capable of accurately accounting for the finite thermal conductivity of the walls. A few final comments and suggestions for future work should be issued. First, we believe that there is room for improvement for RANS models. Here, a simple Spalart-Allmaras model was tested, but it would be worth verifying the accuracy of more elaborate models based on full Reynolds stress closures. In fact, we expect that full Reynolds stress models can correctly predict the presence of secondary motions, with incurred benefits in terms of realism in the turbulent transport of momentum and heat. Second, we believe that classical formulas based on the assumption of uniform heating should be used with great caution in CHT problems, as coupling with heat conduction in the solid phase yields large nonuniformity which in turn imply reduction of the overall system cooling performance. Last, we believe that additional computational and experimental studies should be carried out to achieve a better characterization of cooling channels (here, of rectangular shape) as a function of non-symmetric heat loading conditions, as existing correlations do not provide sufficient predictive accuracy.
